</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">email: </span><a href="mailto:mengzhu.jyn@taobao.com">mengzhu.jyn@taobao.com</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">2018</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Modern recommender systems leverage ultra-long user behavior sequences to capture dynamic preferences, but end-to-end modeling is infeasible in production due to latency and memory constraints. While summarizing history via interest centers offers a practical alternative, existing methods struggle to (1) identify user-specific centers at appropriate granularity and (2) accurately assign behaviors, leading to quantization errors and loss of long-tail preferences. To alleviate these issues, we propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hierarchical Sparse Activation Compression</span> (HiSAC), an efficient framework for personalized sequence modeling. HiSAC encodes interactions into multi-level semantic IDs and constructs a global hierarchical codebook. A hierarchical voting mechanism sparsely activates personalized <em id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">interest-agents</em> as fine-grained preference centers. Guided by these agents, Soft-Routing Attention aggregates historical signals in semantic space, weighting by similarity to minimize quantization error and retain long-tail behaviors. Deployed on Taobao’s “Guess What You Like” homepage, HiSAC achieves significant compression and cost reduction, with online A/B tests showing a consistent <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.65%</span> CTR uplift—demonstrating its scalability and real-world effectiveness.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Long Sequence Modeling, Recommender System.
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Modern recommender systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, widely deployed in domains such as e-commerce and video streaming, rely on user behavior sequences to capture evolving preferences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pi et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
For highly active users, these histories can contain thousands of interactions, reflecting both short-term and long-term dynamics.
While such ultra-long sequences provide rich signals for personalization, directly modeling them in production environments is computationally expensive and memory-intensive, often driving inference latency beyond acceptable limits in real-time serving.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Although many strategies have been explored for compressing long behavior sequences, substantial limitations persist in practical recommendation scenarios.
For instance, ELASTIC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and PolyEncoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Humeau et al., 2019</a>)</cite> transform each user’s behaviors into a fixed set of <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">global</em> interest embeddings, overlooking that users vary widely in both the number and granularity of their interest centers.
Other grouping approaches—such as temporal segmentation, locality-sensitive hashing (LSH) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kitaev et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, and K-Means clustering—are often unstable and sensitive to hyperparameters: temporal splitting ignores semantic proximity, while hash- and cluster-based techniques can misassign semantically related behaviors to different groups.
These issues highlight two fundamental challenges:
(1) accurately identifying user-specific interest centers, whose number and granularity differ across individuals; and
(2) reliably assigning historical behaviors to the most compatible interest center to reduce quantization error and preserve semantic consistency.
Deficiencies in the second aspect naturally lead to loss of rare yet valuable long-tail preferences, diminishing recommendation diversity and overall quality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we propose <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hierarchical Sparse Activation Compression</span> (HiSAC),
a framework for ultra-long sequence modeling that suppresses noise and attention dilution while maintaining stable, semantically consistent interest grouping.
<span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Firstly</span>, HiSAC tokenizes user histories into discrete multi-level semantic identifiers via a large-scale multimodal encoder
(integrating product text and images) and a Residual Quantized Variational Autoencoder (RQ-VAE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
<span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Secondly</span>, a global hierarchical semantic tree is built and a <em id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hierarchical voting mechanism</em> then aligns each user’s tokenized sequence to this hierarchy,
pruning and retaining the most representative branches to produce personalized <em id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">interest-agents</em>
capturing both coarse- and fine-grained preference centers.
<span id="S1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Finally</span>, guided by these agents, <em id="S1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Soft-Routing Attention</em> aggregates signals from past interactions in the learned semantic space,
weighting contributions by similarity to reduce quantization error <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Van Den Oord et al., 2017</a>)</cite>
and preserve infrequent yet valuable long-tail behaviors.
The resulting compact, expressive user representation accelerates retrieval and ranking, lowers serving costs,
and—in industrial-scale deployment—yields a consistent <span id="S1.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.65%</span> CTR uplift over the strongest prior compression method.
Our main contributions are:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design a hierarchical voting mechanism for sparse activation of user interests, built upon a multimodal RQ-VAE. This mechanism generates user-specific interest-agents that represent the primary centers of user interest.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We develop a Soft-Routing Attention mechanism to reliably assign behaviors, minimize quantization error, and inherently preserve long-tail preferences.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We validate HiSAC in industrial-scale deployment, demonstrating reduced computational and serving costs alongside a 1.65% CTR uplift.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sequence Modeling in Recommendation.</span>
Modeling user interaction sequences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> is central to modern recommender systems, enabling fine-grained tracking of dynamic preferences.
Early attention-based models like DIN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2018</a>)</cite> leveraged recent behaviors to capture short-term interests, achieving notable gains.
Later work recognized that longer histories offer a richer view of user preferences, yet processing ultra-long sequences incurs high computational, memory, and latency costs.
To mitigate this, two-stage methods such as SIM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pi et al., 2020</a>)</cite> and TWIN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Si et al., 2024</a>)</cite> first retrieve a subset of behaviors before ranking, effectively shortening the sequence.
While efficient, these approaches risk retrieval–ranking inconsistency and discard potentially valuable behavioral signals.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sequence Compression for Ultra-long Histories.</span>
To improve efficiency, various sequence compression strategies have been explored. ELASTIC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and PolyEncoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Humeau et al., 2019</a>)</cite> compress sequences into a fixed set of global interest embeddings, offering computational savings but ignoring diversity in <em id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">user-specific interest granularity</em>. Locality-sensitive hashing (LSH) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kitaev et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, K-Means clustering, and temporal segmentation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chai et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liao et al., 2025</a>)</cite> have also been used for grouping behaviors, but temporal splitting disregards semantic proximity, and clustering- or hash-based methods can suffer from instability and misassignment, undermining personalization and discarding valuable long-tail signals.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quantized Representation Learning.</span>
Vector Quantized Variational Autoencoders (VQ-VAE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Van Den Oord et al., 2017</a>)</cite> introduce discrete codebooks to learn compact latent representations, defining quantization error as the <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> distance between encoder outputs and selected codebook vectors. Extensions such as VQ-VAE-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Razavi et al., 2019</a>)</cite> employ multi-level quantization, while Residual Quantized VAE (RQ-VAE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2022</a>)</cite> progressively quantizes residuals to reduce total quantization error. These approaches have proved effective in domains like vision and speech, and their principles can be adapted to recommender systems for scalable sequence modeling.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our method combines multimodal RQ-VAE tokenization, hierarchical voting, and soft-routing attention to identify user-specific interest centers. Unlike global fixed embeddings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Humeau et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, HiSAC learns personalized interest agents, reduces quantization error, preserves long-tail preferences, and scales to industrial deployment.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21009v1/model.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/210;" width="452" height="210" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>Overview of HiSAC, which compresses ultra-long user sequences in three stages:
(a) tokenization of historical behaviors;
(b) sparse activation by hierarchical voting for interest-agents selection;
(c) soft-routing attention to produce the compressed representation.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Method</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Problem Formulation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a recommender system with an item set <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> and a set of users <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, each user <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> has an interaction history represented as an ordered sequence:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{u}=[h_{1},\dots,h_{n},\dots,h_{N}],\quad h_{n}\in\mathcal{I}\text{ and }1\leq n\leq N," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{u}=[h_{1},\dots,h_{n},\dots,h_{N}],\quad h_{n}\in\mathcal{I}\text{ and }1\leq n\leq N,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the sequence length. The goal of the ranking model <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> in the recommender system is to estimate the probability <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> that user <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> will interact with a target item <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., click, purchase, like and so on), conditioned on the historical sequence <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For highly active users, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> can be extremely large, sometimes exceeding <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> interactions, which makes direct sequence modeling computationally expensive and poses significant scalability challenges in industrial applications.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this, we aim to construct a compressed representation of user interests:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{H}_{u}=[\hat{z}_{1},\dots,\hat{z}_{K}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{H}_{u}=[\hat{z}_{1},\dots,\hat{z}_{K}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where each <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z}_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{d^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z}_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a summary of a distinct aspect of the user’s interests, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\ll N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\ll N</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the length of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{H}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{H}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>. Crucially, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{H}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{H}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a subsequence of items from <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, but rather a compact set of interest-level representations that capture diverse and informative behavioral patterns.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Overview of Method</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed method is designed to model ultra-long user behavior sequences by mitigating noise and attention dilution, whose structure is shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2. Related Work ‣ HiSAC: Hierarchical Sparse Activation Compression for Ultra-long Sequence Modeling in Recommenders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
It compresses sequences in three stages: (a) tokenizing historical behaviors, (b) hierarchical voting for sparse activating coarse- and fine-grained interest agents, and (c) soft-routing attention to produce a compact, expressive representation.
Beyond these core components, we also provide details on its industrial-scale deployment, demonstrating HiSAC’s practicality in real-world recommendation systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Tokenization of User Historical Behaviors</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In modern recommender systems, item representations are typically learned end-to-end, effectively capturing task-specific patterns but often inheriting biases from item popularity and exposure frequency—compromising fairness and generalization. To mitigate this, we employ a large-scale multimodal encoder to generate embeddings for all items in the corpus <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math>. By jointly processing heterogeneous modalities, the encoder integrates complementary semantic cues while reducing biases inherent in interaction-driven learning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2. Related Work ‣ HiSAC: Hierarchical Sparse Activation Compression for Ultra-long Sequence Modeling in Recommenders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>-(a), for an item <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math>, we collect its descriptive metadata-including title, category labels, and domain-specific text—along with its product image. These multimodal signals are processed by an encoder that aligns and fuses them via modality-specific encoders and shared fusion layers. The resulting embedding unifies visual and textual semantics in a common space, enabling effective retrieval and ranking. By decoupling item representation learning from the recommendation model’s optimization, our approach mitigates exposure bias and improves embedding generalization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Although multimodal-based embeddings offer rich semantic information, they reside in a continuous feature space where clustering behavior can be ambiguous and direct grouping of items may incur substantial quantization errors.
To mitigate these issues, we employ the Residual Quantized Variational Autoencoder (RQ-VAE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2022</a>)</cite> to discretize the continuous embeddings <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}</annotation></semantics></math> into a <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-level hierarchy of codebooks.
RQ-VAE applies residual quantization: the first level directly quantizes the encoder output, while each subsequent level iteratively quantizes the residual from the preceding level. The implementation details of multimodal encoding and RQ-VAE are available at Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Implementation Details of Multimodal Encoding and RQ-VAE ‣ HiSAC: Hierarchical Sparse Activation Compression for Ultra-long Sequence Modeling in Recommenders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Using the learned codebook, each item <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is mapped to an <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-level <em id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">semantic identifier</em> (SID) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, and the user’s historical interaction sequence <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> is correspondingly tokenized into the semantic space. This process preserves semantic structure while yielding a compact, discrete representation that facilitates efficient interest modeling and candidate matching.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>Sparse Activation by Hierarchical Voting</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To address the first challenge in Section <a href="#S1" title="1. Introduction ‣ HiSAC: Hierarchical Sparse Activation Compression for Ultra-long Sequence Modeling in Recommenders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, accurately identifying user-specific interest centers, we construct a set of <em id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">interest-agents</em> for each user via a hierarchical voting mechanism that derives agents from the tokenized sequence in two steps as shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2. Related Work ‣ HiSAC: Hierarchical Sparse Activation Compression for Ultra-long Sequence Modeling in Recommenders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>-(b):</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 1: Building Global Hierarchical Semantic Tree</span>.
We first organize the entire SID space into a global <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-level tree, where each node at level <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a codeword index in the <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>-th codebook. The root (level 0) is a virtual node connected to all level-1 nodes. A path from the root to a leaf (level <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>) uniquely defines a complete SID, thereby encoding a fine-grained semantic concept. This static tree serves as a shared semantic index across all users and enables efficient hierarchical aggregation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 2: Voting for Interest-Agents</span>.
Given a user’s tokenized history, we project each item onto its corresponding leaf node. We then perform a bottom-up vote propagation: <span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1)</span> Each leaf node receives a vote count equal to the number of items mapped to it; <span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2)</span> For internal nodes, the vote count is the sum of votes from all its descendants. Starting from the root, we traverse the tree top-down. At each level <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in\{1,\dots,L\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in\{1,\dots,L\}</annotation></semantics></math>, for every activated parent node (initially only the root), we retain only the top-<math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> child nodes with the highest vote counts and deactivate the rest. This layer-wise sparsification ensures that computational resources are focused on the most salient semantic branches that best reflect the user’s dominant interests.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The remaining active leaf nodes after pruning define the user’s <em id="S3.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">interest-agents</em>:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{u}=\{a_{1},\dots,a_{K}\},\quad\mathcal{W}_{u}=\{w_{a,1},\dots,w_{a,K}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{u}=\{a_{1},\dots,a_{K}\},\quad\mathcal{W}_{u}=\{w_{a,1},\dots,w_{a,K}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">each agent <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by its full root-to-leaf path (which is an <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-level SID). <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes weight of <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, computed as the softmax-normalized count of items assigned to each agent, and reflects the strength of the corresponding interest. <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\prod_{l=1}^{L}k^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\prod_{l=1}^{L}k^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the size of <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>. This sparse, adaptive selection avoids combinatorial explosion and yields a compact yet expressive interest summary suitable for downstream modeling.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5. </span>Soft-Routing Attention for Compression</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Once <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{u}=\{a_{1},\dots,a_{K}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{u}=\{a_{1},\dots,a_{K}\}</annotation></semantics></math> has been determined, a common approach is <span id="S3.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hard-Routing</span>, which assigns each historical interaction to its corresponding interest agent via exact SID matching. However, such hard assignment can amplify quantization errors in discrete interest representations and fails to utilize long-tail interactions—defined here as items whose SIDs do not match any activated agent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address these limitations, we propose a <em id="S3.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">soft-routing attention</em> mechanism that allows each interest agent to aggregate information from <em id="S3.SS5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">all</em> historical interactions in a similarity-aware manner. Our key insight is: <span id="S3.SS5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">routing decisions should be based on debiased semantic similarity, while the aggregated representation must remain aligned with the downstream ranking objective</span>. This leads us to leverage two distinct item embeddings:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Semantic embedding</span> <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐞</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>:
Produced by the frozen multimodal encoder and RQ-VAE (Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3. Tokenization of User Historical Behaviors ‣ 3. Method ‣ HiSAC: Hierarchical Sparse Activation Compression for Ultra-long Sequence Modeling in Recommenders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>), this embedding captures intrinsic cross-modal semantics (e.g., visual appearance and textual attributes) and is <em id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not updated</em> during recommendation training. It is used solely to compute similarity between items and interest agents.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ranking embedding</span> <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{d^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐞</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>: Learned end-to-end within the recommendation model (e.g., via CTR or ranking loss), this embedding captures interaction-driven signals—such as user preferences and collaborative patterns—and is concatenated with item-side features (e.g., category, brand). Additionally, a time-decay feature, which quantifies the temporal distance between each historical interaction and the current request timestamp, is incorporated as side information. The resulting representation is <em id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">trainable</em> and serves as the content embedding for subsequent aggregation.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For each interest agent <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, its semantic prototype <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{a}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{a}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained by summing the corresponding codewords from all <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> levels of the RQ-VAE codebooks.
Note that <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> resides in the same embedding space as <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, enabling direct computation of the <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> distance.
Intuitively, a smaller <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> distance between <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates higher semantic similarity, and thus should correspond to a larger attention weight.
Therefore, we compute soft routing weights using the negative <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> distances between <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> and each <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, normalized via a temperature-controlled softmax:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\hat{z}}_{k}=w_{a,k}\cdot\sum_{i=1}^{N}\underbrace{\frac{\exp\left(-\frac{\left\|\mathbf{e}_{i}-\mathbf{z}_{a,k}\right\|_{2}}{\tau}\right)}{\sum_{j=1}^{N}\exp\left(-\frac{\left\|\mathbf{e}_{j}-\mathbf{z}_{a,k}\right\|_{2}}{\tau}\right)}}_{\text{semantic based attention weight}}\cdot\mathbf{e}^{\prime}_{i}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>τ</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>τ</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>semantic based attention weight</mtext></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\hat{z}}_{k}=w_{a,k}\cdot\sum_{i=1}^{N}\underbrace{\frac{\exp\left(-\frac{\left\|\mathbf{e}_{i}-\mathbf{z}_{a,k}\right\|_{2}}{\tau}\right)}{\sum_{j=1}^{N}\exp\left(-\frac{\left\|\mathbf{e}_{j}-\mathbf{z}_{a,k}\right\|_{2}}{\tau}\right)}}_{\text{semantic based attention weight}}\cdot\mathbf{e}^{\prime}_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> controls the sharpness of the distribution. The final agent representation <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is thus a weighted combination of ranking embeddings, with weights derived from debiased semantic similarity. This design effectively mitigates quantization artifacts and preserves signals from long-tail interactions. <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{a,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{a,k}</annotation></semantics></math> is used to mark the strength of the interest.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we define the compressed sequence as <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{H}_{u}=\{\hat{z}_{1},\dots\,\hat{z}_{K}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{H}_{u}=\{\hat{z}_{1},\dots\,\hat{z}_{K}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The routing computation admits an efficient matrix implementation analogous to attention mechanisms, which is detailed in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Matrix Formulation of Soft-Routing Attention ‣ HiSAC: Hierarchical Sparse Activation Compression for Ultra-long Sequence Modeling in Recommenders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21009v1/mha.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:205/132;" width="205" height="132" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>Comparison of industrial deployment architectures.
(a) Standard HiSAC combined with multi-head attention (MHA).
(b) Optimized deployment incorporating online and offline cache for reduced latency and cost.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.6. </span>Online Deployment</h3>

<div id="S3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We deploy HiSAC with multi-head attention (MHA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al., 2017</a>)</cite> for cross modeling between compressed user sequences and candidate items, achieving an optimal balance between accuracy and latency in large-scale production.
As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.5. Soft-Routing Attention for Compression ‣ 3. Method ‣ HiSAC: Hierarchical Sparse Activation Compression for Ultra-long Sequence Modeling in Recommenders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we further apply three optimizations:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) Offline Interest-Agent Construction.</span>
For ultra-long sequences, a user’s primary interests typically remain stable over short time horizons. Leveraging this property, we construct each user’s <em id="S3.SS6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">interest tree</em> offline once per day, avoiding repeated computation online.
Specifically, a multimodal encoder with Residual RQ-VAE initializes the item corpus, which is updated daily for newly added items.
Historical behaviors are tokenized into semantic IDs, followed by personalized interest-tree building and interest-agent extraction.
These are stored and directly retrieved during online inference, effectively reducing latency and improving runtime efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) Request-Level Compression.</span>
In serving, direct MHA between <math id="S3.SS6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> sequence tokens and <math id="S3.SS6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> candidate items incurs <math id="S3.SS6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(NM)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(NM)</annotation></semantics></math> complexity.
Since the user’s sequence remains unchanged for all candidates within a request, we compress it from <math id="S3.SS6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> tokens once per request (<math id="S3.SS6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(NK)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(NK)</annotation></semantics></math>).
The compressed sequence is then used in MHA-based cross modeling with the <math id="S3.SS6.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> candidates (<math id="S3.SS6.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(KM)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(KM)</annotation></semantics></math>), significantly lowering the total cost <math id="S3.SS6.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(NK)+\mathcal{O}(KM)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(NK)+\mathcal{O}(KM)</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.SS6.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\ll N,M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\ll N,M</annotation></semantics></math>.
This optimization achieves substantial latency reduction without affecting recommendation accuracy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3) Cache-Enhanced MHA.</span>
Profiling shows over 70% of serving-side FLOPs come from query/key/value linear projections in MHA.
Moreover, for most requests, as the interest-agents remain stable within a short time window and candidate items largely retain static semantic features, we decompose the MHA computation into three parts:</p>
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Candidate queries</span>: Pre-computed offline from static features and stored in a high-throughput cache, removing online query projection overhead.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">User keys/values</span>: Computed once after sequence compression per request and cached for reuse within the same request window.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Attention execution</span>: Cached queries and keys/values are loaded to perform attention scoring and aggregation.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.SS6.p4.2" class="ltx_p">This cache-oriented design preserves identical outputs to vanilla MHA while reducing projection FLOPs by <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 72\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>72</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 72\%</annotation></semantics></math> and lowering per-request inference time by <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="38\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>38</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">38\%</annotation></semantics></math>, with hit rates above <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="99\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>99</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">99\%</annotation></semantics></math> in our production benchmark.
Overall, these deployment optimizations jointly enable HiSAC to operate under industrial real-time constraints, with over 40% end-to-end latency reduction and no measurable loss in recommendation performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1. </span>Results on the both Datasets.</figcaption>
<div id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:465.0pt;height:155.7pt;vertical-align:-75.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-41.0pt,13.7pt) scale(0.8,0.8) ;">
<table id="S3.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">Dataset</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" colspan="8"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Industrial Dataset</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_tt" colspan="2"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Taobao-MM</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Sequence Length</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" colspan="2">1k</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" colspan="2">2k</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" colspan="2">4k</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_t" colspan="2">10k</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_t" colspan="2">1k</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Method</th>
