<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Setup</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.1. </span><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset</span>.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our method on two datasets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Industrial Dataset</span>: We constructed a large-scale industrial dataset using authentic, high-quality user interaction logs collected from Taobao, one of the world’s largest e-commerce platforms. The logs were continuously recorded over a 14-day period, with several billion interaction events generated daily. In total, the dataset contains approximately 200 million users, 1 billion items, and about 30 billion interaction records. The maximum sequence length per user is capped at 10,000. Data from the first 13 days is used for training, while the final day serves as the validation set.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Taobao-MM<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">1</span></span>
                  
                  
                  
                <span id="footnote1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">https://huggingface.co/datasets/TaoBao-MM/Taobao-MM</span></span></span></span></span>: Taobao-MM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> is an officially released multi-modal dataset provided by Taobao. For our experiments, we use all 8.86M users and 275 million items. User-side features include user ID, age, gender, while item-side features consist of item ID and category. The maximum allowed sequence length for this dataset is 1,000.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.2. </span><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics</span>.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In offline experiments, we adopt three standard metrics widely used in recommendation systems. We adopt the Area Under the ROC Curve (AUC) and Group AUC (GAUC). GAUC is calculated as the average of per-user AUCs, which mitigates the influence of highly active users and better reflects the typical user experience.
In online experiments, we evaluate two categories of metrics:
utility and user engagement. Utility metrics quantify the recommendation system’s ability to drive user actions and downstream revenue. We primarily track Item Page View (IPV), Click-Through Rate (CTR), Click-Through Conversion Rate (CTCVR) and Orders.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.3. </span><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compared Methods &amp; Implementation Details</span>.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by presenting several representative and widely adopted sequence compression techniques. These methods are combined with multi-head attention (MHA) to facilitate user sequence modeling:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">K-Means</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chong et al., 2021</a>)</cite>: As adopted in the Routing Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Roy et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, this approach clusters tokens into dynamic groups based on feature similarity, allowing intra-cluster attention and providing a stable, task-adaptive, similarity-driven compression mechanism.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LSH</span>: The locality-sensitive hashing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Datar et al., 2004</a>)</cite> mechanism used in Reformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kitaev et al., 2020</a>)</cite> buckets similar tokens together, enabling attention computation within buckets and thereby reducing complexity while maintaining semantic proximity in long user histories.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Patching</span>: Groups historical items according to their timestamps, preserving temporal ordering and enabling time-aware compression of behavioral sequences.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Aggregator</span>: The method leverages a set of learnable tokens to compress user behavior sequences, following a similar design philosophy to ELASTIC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and PolyEncoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Humeau et al., 2019</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We further integrate our proposed method with several state-of-the-art (SOTA) approaches widely adopted in the industry to validate its effectiveness.
These approaches include:</p>
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PatchRec<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liao et al., 2025</a><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span>: The method divides the user’s behavioral history into sequential blocks according to temporal order, performs pooling within each block to produce its representation, and employs different block sizes to separately model long-term and short-term behaviors.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ELASTIC<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2024</a><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span>: In this approach, a fixed-size set of representation vectors is learned. The user’s behavior sequence is then compressed by applying cross-attention that aligns each behavior with the most relevant representation in the set.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Longer<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chai et al., 2025</a><span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span>: This method partitions the user’s behaviors into evenly spaced short sequences according to chronological order. Each short sequence is then compressed into a final representation vector using Linear Attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, thereby reducing the overall sequence length.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">All models are trained using the Adam optimizer for dense parameters and Adagrad for sparse parameters, with an initial learning rate of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the Industrial Dataset, the input features comprise user ID, gender, item ID, item category, and the user behavior sequence.
Item information is encoded by a frozen CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, while the RQ-VAE is configured with 2 quantization layers, each containing a codebook of 512 entries.
For fairness, all compared methods compress the user sequence into 200 groups.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For Taobao-MM Dataset we adopt SCL-based multimodal embeddings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sheng et al., 2024</a>)</cite> to train the RQ-VAE, which is configured with a codebook size of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>200</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200\times 2</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., 200 entries per layer across 2 layers). The input features include user ID, age, gender, item ID, item category, and the user behavior sequence.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Overall Performance Comparison</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we compare the performance of HiSAC against all baseline methods on both datasets, the user sequence is compressed into 200 groups. The results are illustrated in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.6. Online Deployment ‣ 3. Method ‣ HiSAC: Hierarchical Sparse Activation Compression for Ultra-long Sequence Modeling in Recommenders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compared with Different Compression Methods.</span> We adopt a simplified MHA baseline, where the candidate item acts as the query and the compressed historical sequence serves as both key and value. Results show that Patching compression performs worst, as it fails to preserve semantic relationships among items. K-Means clustering improves performance via explicit modeling of inter-item similarity, but its iterative updates incur high computational cost and leave notable quantization artifacts. LSH alleviates complexity while maintaining reasonable clustering quality, though inherent randomness in hashing introduces performance variance. The Aggregator mechanism yields additional gains by learning shared user-interest representations, but sacrifices personalization. In contrast, our HiSAC method, introducing interest-agents and Soft-Routing Attention, captures fine-grained personalized interests while mitigating quantization error, achieving the best overall performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Robustness and Generalizability Across SOTA Models.</span>
To rigorously assess the robustness and generalizability of our framework, we integrate HiSAC into several representative state-of-the-art (SOTA) sequence modeling architectures that employ sequence compression. Specifically, in each architecture, the original compression or grouping module is replaced with HiSAC, while all other components remain unchanged. For fair comparison, the number of compressed groups is fixed at 200—consistent with HiSAC+MHA. Results show that these SOTA baselines already outperform the plain MHA model; however, their compression mechanisms exhibit suboptimal inductive bias, limiting their ability to fully capture sequential information. Replacing them with HiSAC yields more semantically coherent groupings that better align with personalized user interests, thereby unlocking latent modeling capacity and consistently improving both AUC and GAUC across all methods.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.21009v1/len.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/72;" width="215" height="72"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3. </span>Sequence length scaling on model performance.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sequence Length Scaling Analysis.</span>
We evaluate six methods across varying sequence lengths to assess their ability to leverage long-range user interaction histories (Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.2. Overall Performance Comparison ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ HiSAC: Hierarchical Sparse Activation Compression for Ultra-long Sequence Modeling in Recommenders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
Plain Multi-Head Attention (MHA) shows moderate gains with increasing length but declines beyond 10k, indicating that attention dilution and noise accumulation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al., 2023</a>)</cite> impair performance.
In contrast, compression-based methods consistently improve as sequences grow, as reduced input length and noise suppression alleviate attention dilution.
HiSAC achieves the largest gain, effectively exploiting long-range context without saturation, whereas Patching plateaus with longer sequences.
Aggregator also benefits but is limited by insufficient representation capacity and poor preservation of fine-grained temporal patterns.
Overall, sequence compression mitigates the drawbacks of long histories, with HiSAC maximizing information retention and delivering the best performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Ablation Study</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the individual contribution of each core component in HiSAC, we perform an ablation study under the same experimental setting as the main evaluation, where the sequence length is fixed at 10,000.
By systematically removing or substituting specific modules, we quantitatively analyze their impact on recommendation performance using the industrial-scale dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I3" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S4.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">w/o Multimodal Encoder</span>: The multimodal item embeddings are replaced with embeddings learned directly within the recommender model, while maintaining the same embedding dimensionality for fairness.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">w/o Text Information</span>: The textual metadata of each item (e.g., title, category) is removed from the multimodal encoder input, and only the corresponding product image is used.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">w/o Product Image</span>: The product image of each item is removed from the multimodal encoder input, and only textual metadata is used.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">w/o RQ-VAE</span>: The RQ-VAE codebook generation is substituted with RQ-KMeans, maintaining the same number of quantization levels and identical codebook size per level to ensure comparability.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">w/o Soft-Routing Attention</span>: Soft-Routing Attention for sequence compression is replaced with Hard-Routing, where each interaction is mapped to its corresponding semantic ID. The representation dimensionality is kept identical across both approaches.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">w/o Long-Tail Interests</span>: In this variant, historical interactions that cannot be matched to any interest agent are discarded, and Soft-Routing Attention is subsequently applied for sequence compression.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">w/o Hierarchical Voting</span>: For this experiment, all user interests are preserved in the personal interest-tree without applying the hierarchical voting mechanism.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2. </span>The Ablation Results on Tokenization</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T2.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:184.9pt;height:62.9pt;vertical-align:-29.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-16.3pt,5.6pt) scale(0.8,0.8) ;">
