<td id="S4.T2.2.1.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.6.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.5525</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3. </span>The Ablation Results on Compression</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T3.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:194.1pt;height:52.7pt;vertical-align:-24.2pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-17.1pt,4.7pt) scale(0.8,0.8) ;">
<table id="S4.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">Methods</th>
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">AUC</th>
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">GAUC</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T3.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">w/o Soft-Routing Attention</th>
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">0.6429</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.5515</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r">w/o Long-Tail Interests</th>
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">0.6438</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.5523</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.2.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">w/o Hierarchical Voting</th>
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.6445</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.5525</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.2.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HiSAC</span></th>
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">0.6444</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.5.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.5525</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ablation on Tokenization Components.</span>
As illustrated in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.3. Ablation Study ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ HiSAC: Hierarchical Sparse Activation Compression for Ultra-long Sequence Modeling in Recommenders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we conduct ablation studies on two key components of HiSAC’s tokenization pipeline. First, removing the Multimodal Encoder leads to a <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.2</annotation></semantics></math>pt <span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            pt = percentage points (absolute difference in percentages).</span></span></span> AUC drop, confirming that semantically enriched item representations—beyond popularity cues—are essential for capturing nuanced user preferences. Within this encoder, textual metadata and product images provide complementary signals: ablating either modality (<span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w/o Text Information</span> or <span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w/o Product Image</span>) degrades performance by <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.15</annotation></semantics></math>pt and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.13</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.13</annotation></semantics></math>pt in AUC, respectively, with text showing slightly stronger impact due to its richer contextual discriminability in our industrial setting. Second, replacing RQ-VAE with RQ-KMeans reduces AUC by <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.06" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.06</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.06</annotation></semantics></math>pt, as direct clustering of raw embeddings yields less distinct clusters and higher quantization error. Cluster analysis corroborates this: RQ-VAE achieves a higher Silhouette Coefficient <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dinh et al., 2019</a>)</cite> (<math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.42" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.42</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.42</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.28" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.28</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.28</annotation></semantics></math>) and lower reconstruction error <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Van Den Oord et al., 2017</a>)</cite> (<math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.018" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.018</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.018</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.027" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.027</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.027</annotation></semantics></math>), indicating that its learned latent space enhances cluster separability and codebook coherence, ultimately improving recommendation accuracy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analysis of Soft-Routing Attention.</span>
We attribute the gains of Soft-Routing Attention to two primary factors in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.3. Ablation Study ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ HiSAC: Hierarchical Sparse Activation Compression for Ultra-long Sequence Modeling in Recommenders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>:
(i) reduced quantization error through soft assignment of interactions to interest agents, and
(ii) effective utilization of users’ long-tail interests.
To verify these effects, we conduct two targeted ablations.
Both <span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w/o Soft-Routing Attention</span> and <span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w/o Long-Tail Interests</span> exclude long-tail interests; however, the latter employs Soft-Routing Attention and achieves an AUC increase of <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.09" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.09</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.09</annotation></semantics></math>pt over <span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w/o Soft-Routing Attention</span>, indicating its role in reducing quantization error.
Incorporating long-tail interests into HiSAC yields a further AUC improvement of <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.06" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.06</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.06</annotation></semantics></math>pt, confirming that long-tail interactions contain valuable signals that can be exploited to enhance recommendation performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ablation on Hierarchical Voting.</span>
To assess the impact of <span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hierarchical Voting</span>, we evaluate a variant that retains all user interests as <span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">interest-agents</span> without voting-based selection. As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.3. Ablation Study ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ HiSAC: Hierarchical Sparse Activation Compression for Ultra-long Sequence Modeling in Recommenders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, this configuration yields only a 0.01pt AUC gain, indicating that indiscriminate retention provides negligible improvement in discriminative capability. Meanwhile, the average number of <span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">interest-agents</span> per user triples (about 300% of baseline), causing a proportional increase in online computational cost. These results confirm that <span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hierarchical Voting</span>, coupled with soft-routing, effectively distills dominant user interests and significantly reduces computation without sacrificing predictive performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4. </span>Analysis of the two space embeddings.</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T4.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:190.4pt;height:42.2pt;vertical-align:-19.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-16.8pt,3.7pt) scale(0.8,0.8) ;">
<table id="S4.T4.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T4.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">Methods</th>
<th id="S4.T4.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">AUC</th>
<th id="S4.T4.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">GAUC</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T4.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T4.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Both Semantic Embedding</th>
<td id="S4.T4.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">0.6253</td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.5392</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T4.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r">Alignment</th>
<td id="S4.T4.2.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">0.6365</td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.5463</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.2.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T4.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T4.2.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decoupling</span></th>
<td id="S4.T4.2.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T4.2.1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.6404</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T4.2.1.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.5491</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analysis of Semantic and Ranking Embeddings</span>.
A core principle of HiSAC is the explicit decoupling of semantic and ranking representations. We use frozen semantic embeddings <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>—derived from RQ-VAE—to compute soft-routing attention weights, while distinct, trainable ranking embeddings <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> serve as aggregation content. To validate this design, we evaluate three variants on a large-scale industrial dataset (Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.3. Ablation Study ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ HiSAC: Hierarchical Sparse Activation Compression for Ultra-long Sequence Modeling in Recommenders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
<span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">First</span>, using the same <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for both routing and aggregation drops AUC by <span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.5</span>pt, revealing that semantic embeddings lack task-specific signals needed for effective ranking. <span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Second</span>, an alternative inspired by He et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2025</a>)</cite> aligns the two embeddings via an auxiliary loss (applied only to the item embedding; side information is trained end-to-end by the ranking model). Though it improves over the single-embedding baseline, it still underperforms our decoupled design.
Critically, this alignment reduces the average number of activated interest agents per user by <span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">13%</span> (from 182 to 158), indicating that co-optimization biases RQ-VAE codebooks toward dominant patterns and suppresses diverse semantic pathways. This collapse in interest coverage particularly harms users with heterogeneous preferences, degrading ranking performance.
<span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Finally</span>, our decoupled strategy preserves semantic diversity—enabling unbiased, broad-coverage routing—while leveraging expressive, task-aware ranking embeddings for prediction. This separation of concerns allows HiSAC to balance semantic fidelity and ranking efficacy, achieving superior overall performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Ablations show HiSAC’s components are vital: multimodal RQ-VAE tokenization outperforms unimodal or KMeans variants; soft-routing attention reduces quantization error and captures long-tail interests; hierarchical voting cuts agent count by 2/3 with negligible loss; and decoupling semantic/ranking embeddings prevents codebook bias, preserving interest diversity (13% higher coverage). Shared embeddings harm heterogeneous users. Together, these enable accurate, efficient, and robust recommendation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4. </span>Hyperparameters Analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To gain deeper insights into the proposed HiSAC framework, we conduct ablation studies on key hyperparameters and design choices: RQ-VAE quantization depth, number of interest-agents, top-<math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> voting strategy, and soft-routing temperature <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.
In all experiments, <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">the RQ-VAE uses <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> codebook levels, each of size 512</span>, ensuring consistent semantic resolution across the hierarchy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of RQ-VAE Levels (<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>) with Controlled Agent Capacity.</span>
To isolate the impact of semantic granularity from agent capacity, we adjust the per-level selection thresholds <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the average number of activated interest agents per user remains approximately 200 across all hierarchy depths. As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.4. Hyperparameters Analysis ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ HiSAC: Hierarchical Sparse Activation Compression for Ultra-long Sequence Modeling in Recommenders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>-(a), both HiSAC and HiSAC-Hard improve when increasing <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> from 1 to 2, confirming the benefit of multi-granular modeling. However, at <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=3</annotation></semantics></math>, HiSAC plateaus while HiSAC-Hard degrades—despite having comparable agent counts. This indicates that the performance drop in HiSAC-Hard stems from increased quantization mismatch under finer granularity, not insufficient capacity. HiSAC mitigates this via soft-routing’s ability to recover semantically similar but non-matching interactions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.21009v1/k_1.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/72;" width="215" height="72"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4. </span>Parameter analysis results for Interest-Agents generation, including the impact of RQ-VAE quantization levels,
the number of interest-agents <math id="S4.F4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, the hierarchical voting top-<math id="S4.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> strategy.
Each curve reports model performance under varying parameter values with other settings fixed.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of Hierarchical Budget Allocation.</span>
With the RQ-VAE depth fixed at <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=2</annotation></semantics></math> and the total number of interest agents constrained to 200, we study how to allocate this budget across the hierarchical levels. We compare three allocation strategies: <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k^{(1)},k^{(2)})=[(50,4),(100,2),(200,1)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>50</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>200</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k^{(1)},k^{(2)})=[(50,4),(100,2),(200,1)]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of top nodes retained at level <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>. As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.4. Hyperparameters Analysis ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ HiSAC: Hierarchical Sparse Activation Compression for Ultra-long Sequence Modeling in Recommenders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>-(b), performance consistently improves as more of the agent budget is allocated to the coarser (higher) level (<math id="S4.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math>). This trend is further supported by our empirical observation that, within each level-1 semantic category, user interests are highly concentrated on only a few top level-2 subcategories. Consequently, retaining a large <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math> yields diminishing returns, as most users exhibit sparse activation over fine-grained codes. These findings suggest that, under a constrained agent budget, broad coverage of high-level semantic categories—which capture fundamental user intent—is more beneficial than fine-grained diversification at deeper levels.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of Total Agent Budget (<math id="S4.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>) under Optimal Allocation.</span>
Building on the above insight, we now study how total agent capacity affects performance when using a well-chosen allocation strategy. Fixing <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=2</annotation></semantics></math>, we vary the overall budget to yield average agent counts of approximately <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=100,150,200," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100,150,200</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=100,150,200,</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="250" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>250</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">250</annotation></semantics></math>. Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.4. Hyperparameters Analysis ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ HiSAC: Hierarchical Sparse Activation Compression for Ultra-long Sequence Modeling in Recommenders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>-(c) shows that HiSAC achieves near-optimal performance at <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\approx 200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\approx 200</annotation></semantics></math>, while HiSAC-Hard continues to improve up to <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=250" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>250</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=250</annotation></semantics></math>. This confirms that soft-routing is more sample-efficient: it effectively leverages long-tail interactions through semantic similarity, without requiring exhaustive exact-match coverage. In contrast, hard-routing demands a larger agent set to maintain sufficient recall of historical behaviors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of the Temperature Coefficient <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
We study the softmax temperature <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> in the routing module (Eq. <a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3.5. Soft-Routing Attention for Compression ‣ 3. Method ‣ HiSAC: Hierarchical Sparse Activation Compression for Ultra-long Sequence Modeling in Recommenders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), which governs the sharpness of attention weights: larger <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> yields smoother distributions, while smaller <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> encourages sharper focus on high-similarity interactions.
As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.4. Hyperparameters Analysis ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ HiSAC: Hierarchical Sparse Activation Compression for Ultra-long Sequence Modeling in Recommenders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>-(a) (<math id="S4.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=200</annotation></semantics></math>), performance peaks at <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(\tau)=-0.18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.18</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(\tau)=-0.18</annotation></semantics></math>.
Further analysis (Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.4. Hyperparameters Analysis ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ HiSAC: Hierarchical Sparse Activation Compression for Ultra-long Sequence Modeling in Recommenders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>-(b)) shows that lowering <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> sharpens the attention distribution, concentrating interactions on the most similar interest agents and suppressing less relevant ones—enhancing selectivity and reducing noise. However, if <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is too small, the distribution becomes overly peaked, effectively degenerating into Hard-Routing and harming robustness by ignoring useful contextual signals. The optimal <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> thus balances noise suppression with sufficient softness for adaptive aggregation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.21009v1/tau_auc.svg" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/72;" width="215" height="72"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5. </span>Impact of the temperature coefficient <math id="S4.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> on AUC and the distribution of attention weight.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5. </span>The Online A/B Testing Results.</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T5.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:186.0pt;height:21.4pt;vertical-align:-8.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-16.4pt,1.9pt) scale(0.8,0.8) ;">
