<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Case Study of Soft-Routing Attention</h2>

<figure id="A1.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21009v1/case.png" id="A1.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/137;" width="215" height="137" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6. </span>Soft-routing in action. Behavior_0 (main interest) and Behavior_1 (long-tail) are assigned to different hierarchical interest-agents based on semantic similarity, demonstrating HiSAC’s ability to retain sparse signals.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Figure <a href="#A1.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Appendix A Case Study of Soft-Routing Attention ‣ HiSAC: Hierarchical Sparse Activation Compression for Ultra-long Sequence Modeling in Recommenders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we present a case study demonstrating HiSAC’s ability to model heterogeneous user interests. The user’s dominant behavior (Behavior_0: T-shirt) exhibits strong semantic alignment with a dedicated interest-agent (e.g., Agent_0 representing “topwear”). Consequently, it is assigned a large routing weight to this agent, ensuring accurate and efficient encoding of the core preference. In contrast, the long-tail behavior (Behavior_1: cotton socks) does not have a perfectly matching agent in the codebook—no agent is explicitly dedicated to “socks.” However, HiSAC leverages the frozen multimodal semantic space to identify agents with related semantics, such as Agent_4 (“footwear”), and distributes the behavior across these agents with weights proportional to their semantic similarity. This soft-routing mechanism ensures that even sparse or niche interactions are meaningfully preserved in the final user representation, avoiding the quantization loss inherent in hard-assignment approaches.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Implementation Details of Multimodal Encoding and RQ<span id="A2.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>VAE</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1. </span>Multimodal Encoding</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt a cross-modal representation learning strategy to encode textual metadata and product images into a shared embedding space via a frozen CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2021</a>)</cite>:</p>
<ul id="A2.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The textual metadata of each item, including title, category, and description, is tokenized and processed by the text encoder (text tower) of a pretrained CLIP model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. The encoder outputs a <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional representation denoted as <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{text}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>text</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{text}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The product image is resized to <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="224\times 224" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>224</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>224</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">224\times 224</annotation></semantics></math> pixels, normalized, and processed by the visual encoder (image tower) of the same pretrained CLIP model. The encoder outputs a <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional representation denoted as <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{img}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>img</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{img}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We employ a Q-Former <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite> with <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> learnable query tokens <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{q}\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{q}\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> to integrate text and image features.
The Q-Former applies multi-head cross-attention from <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{text}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>text</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{text}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{img}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>img</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{img}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{Q}=\mathrm{CrossAttn}(Q,\;[x_{\text{text}},x_{\text{img}}])" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>CrossAttn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>text</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>img</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{Q}=\mathrm{CrossAttn}(Q,\;[x_{\text{text}},x_{\text{img}}])</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">followed by mean pooling to produce the fused multimodal embedding <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{\text{mm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mtext>mm</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{\text{mm}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{\text{mm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mtext>mm</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{\text{mm}}</annotation></semantics></math> is then fed into the RQ-VAE module.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Q-Former is optimized to align text and image representations in a shared space using a symmetric InfoNCE objective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2020</a>)</cite>:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{align}}=\mathrm{InfoNCE}(\tilde{Q}\leftrightarrow x_{\text{img}})\;+\;\mathrm{InfoNCE}(\tilde{Q}\leftrightarrow x_{\text{text}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>align</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>InfoNCE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">↔</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>img</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><mi>InfoNCE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">↔</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>text</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{align}}=\mathrm{InfoNCE}(\tilde{Q}\leftrightarrow x_{\text{img}})\;+\;\mathrm{InfoNCE}(\tilde{Q}\leftrightarrow x_{\text{text}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{InfoNCE}(A\leftrightarrow B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>InfoNCE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">↔</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{InfoNCE}(A\leftrightarrow B)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the standard bidirectional contrastive loss between modality <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> over a batch.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2. </span>RQ-VAE Training Procedure</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the fused multimodal embedding <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{\text{mm}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mtext>mm</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{\text{mm}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained from the Q-Former, we employ a Residual Quantized Variational Autoencoder (RQ-VAE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2022</a>)</cite> to discretize and cluster item representations into compact codebook entries:</p>
<ul id="A2.I2" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="A2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The RQ-VAE encoder maps <math id="A2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{\text{mm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mtext>mm</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{\text{mm}}</annotation></semantics></math> into a latent vector <math id="A2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is progressively quantized through <math id="A2.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> residual codebook levels, each codebook containing a series of learnable codewords. At each level <math id="A2.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, the quantized output <math id="A2.I2.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> is added to the residual from the previous level, enabling finer partitioning of the embedding space.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The quantized vector <math id="A2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z}</annotation></semantics></math> (sum of residual quantizations) is passed to the decoder to reconstruct the original multimodal embedding <math id="A2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{e}_{\text{mm}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>mm</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{e}_{\text{mm}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. This ensures that the quantization process preserves semantic fidelity.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The RQ-VAE training objective consists of a reconstruction term and a commitment term applied at each residual quantization level:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{RQ-VAE}}=\underbrace{\|e_{\text{mm}}-\hat{e}_{\text{mm}}\|_{2}^{2}}_{\text{Reconstruction}}+\beta\sum_{l=1}^{L}\underbrace{\|e^{(l)}-\mathrm{sg}(z^{(l)})\|_{2}^{2}}_{\text{Commitment at level $l$}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>RQ-VAE</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mtext>mm</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>mm</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Reconstruction</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>sg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mtext>Commitment at level </mtext><mi mathsize="0.700em">l</mi></mrow></munder></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{RQ-VAE}}=\underbrace{\|e_{\text{mm}}-\hat{e}_{\text{mm}}\|_{2}^{2}}_{\text{Reconstruction}}+\beta\sum_{l=1}^{L}\underbrace{\|e^{(l)}-\mathrm{sg}(z^{(l)})\|_{2}^{2}}_{\text{Commitment at level $l$}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the loss coefficient, which is set to <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.25</annotation></semantics></math> uniformly across all quantization levels following the standard configuration in RQ-VAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the input to the <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>-th residual codebook, obtained by subtracting all previously quantized codewords from the initial encoder output <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</p>
<table id="A2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{(1)}=e,\quad e^{(l)}=e^{(l-1)}-z^{(l-1)},\;l&gt;1;" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{(1)}=e,\quad e^{(l)}=e^{(l-1)}-z^{(l-1)},\;l&gt;1;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">and <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{(l)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{(l)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the quantized codeword selected from the <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>-th codebook via nearest-neighbor search; <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sg}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>sg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sg}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the stop-gradient operation, preventing gradient flow into the codebooks.
We adopt the EMA-based codebook update strategy proposed in VQ-VAE v2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Razavi et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, with a decay factor of 0.99, applied to codeword counts and assigned vector sums.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Matrix Formulation of Soft-Routing Attention</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathcal{A}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathcal{A}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote its quantized embedding by <math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{a}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{a}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here, <math id="A3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the set of interest-agents for user <math id="A3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> obtained via the hierarchical voting mechanism, and <math id="A3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> is the codeword embedding extracted from the RQ-VAE codebook.
The quantized embeddings for all <math id="A3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> interest-agents are organized row-wise using a stack operation:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=\mathrm{Stack}([z_{a,1},\dots,z_{a,K}])\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times d}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Stack</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=\mathrm{Stack}([z_{a,1},\dots,z_{a,K}])\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times d},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Stack}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Stack</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Stack}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> concatenates <math id="A3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> vectors into a matrix, preserving the ordering of agents.
<span id="A3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">In the <em id="A3.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Semantic Space</em></span>, the embeddings of the <math id="A3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> historical items <math id="A3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> are:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=\mathrm{Stack}([e_{1},\dots,e_{N}])\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Stack</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=\mathrm{Stack}([e_{1},\dots,e_{N}])\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the semantic representation of <math id="A3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}\in H_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}\in H_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, produced by the multimodal encoder followed by RQ-VAE.
These preserve rich modality-aware semantics independent of the ranking model.
<span id="A3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">In the <em id="A3.p1.3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Ranking Space</em></span>, where representations are trained end-to-end via the ranking objective, the corresponding item embeddings are:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}=Stack([e^{\prime}_{1},\dots,e^{\prime}_{N}])\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d^{\prime}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}=Stack([e^{\prime}_{1},\dots,e^{\prime}_{N}])\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">These ranking-space embeddings combine semantic, collaborative, and position-dependent signals optimized for recommendation accuracy.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The routing between interest-agents and historical items is established by computing a similarity matrix <math id="A3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times N}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i,j}=-\frac{\|E_{j}-Z_{i}\|_{2}}{\tau}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>τ</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i,j}=-\frac{\|E_{j}-Z_{i}\|_{2}}{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Euclidean distance and <math id="A3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a temperature scaling factor controlling the sharpness of the weight distribution.
The negative sign ensures that smaller distances correspond to larger similarity scores.
Applying <em id="A3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">row-wise</em> softmax to <math id="A3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> yields normalized routing weights directly:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{H}_{u}=\mathcal{W}_{u}\odot(\mathrm{Softmax}(W)\cdot E^{\prime})=[\hat{z}_{1},\dots,\hat{z}_{K}]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{H}_{u}=\mathcal{W}_{u}\odot(\mathrm{Softmax}(W)\cdot E^{\prime})=[\hat{z}_{1},\dots,\hat{z}_{K}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> is the agent weight matrix (as in Eq. <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.4. Sparse Activation by Hierarchical Voting ‣ 3. Method ‣ HiSAC: Hierarchical Sparse Activation Compression for Ultra-long Sequence Modeling in Recommenders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), each <math id="A3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z}_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{d^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z}_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the aggregated ranking-space embedding for the <math id="A3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th interest-agent.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This mechanism mirrors the structure of multi-head attention:
interest-agents act as <em id="A3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">queries</em>, historical items serve as both <em id="A3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">keys</em> and <em id="A3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">values</em>, and attention weights are computed via negative Euclidean distance rather than the standard dot product.
The choice of <math id="A3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> distance emphasizes metric preservation in the learned embedding spaces, producing more stable agent–item routing than cosine similarity in our industrial setting.
Overall, this formulation enables each agent to selectively aggregate signals from semantically related items, thereby improving the fidelity of user modeling.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Comparison with Two-Stage Methods</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Modern recommendation systems increasingly adopt a two-stage architecture to handle long user behavior sequences efficiently.
The first stage, termed the <em id="A4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">General Search Unit</em> (GSU), retrieves a short and relevant subsequence from the full historical behaviors by applying lightweight matching or retrieval mechanisms.
This step acts as a coarse filter, focusing subsequent computation on potentially informative interactions while suppressing noise and reducing sequence length.
The second stage, the <em id="A4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Exact Search Unit</em> (ESU), performs fine-grained user interest modeling on the retrieved subsequence, typically employing attention-based or Transformer architectures.
ESU refines the representation by capturing intricate temporal dependencies
and high-resolution feature interactions most pertinent to the target item.
Together, GSU and ESU form a coarse-to-fine pipeline: GSU efficiently narrows the search space, and ESU leverages the reduced yet relevant history to generate precise representations for final prediction.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Representative implementations of the two-stage paradigm include:</p>
<ul id="A4.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="A4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SIM</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pi et al., 2020</a>)</cite>: A two-stage long-sequence modeling framework based on the GSU–ESU paradigm. It includes two variants: <span id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SIM-Hard</span> and <span id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SIM-Soft</span>. In <span id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SIM-Hard</span>, the GSU stage performs retrieval using the item category as the matching key, whereas <span id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SIM-Soft</span> relies on the cosine similarity between item embeddings. In the ESU stage, multi-head attention (MHA) is applied to perform cross-attention between the retrieved short sequence and the target item, enabling target-aware interaction modeling.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TWIN</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>: In SIM, the retrieval objectives of the GSU and ESU stages are not fully aligned. TWIN addresses this by employing the same retrieval strategy (MHA-based cross-attention) and parameter settings in both stages, thereby enhancing the consistency and coherence of the overall model pipeline.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6. </span>Comparison with Two-Stage Methods.</figcaption>
<table id="A4.T6.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A4.T6.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.T6.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">Methods</th>
<th id="A4.T6.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">AUC</th>
<th id="A4.T6.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">GAUC</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A4.T6.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.T6.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">SIM-Hard</th>
<td id="A4.T6.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">0.6393</td>
<td id="A4.T6.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.5481</td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T6.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.T6.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r">SIM-Soft</th>
<td id="A4.T6.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">0.6398</td>
<td id="A4.T6.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.5484</td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T6.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.T6.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r">TWIN</th>
<td id="A4.T6.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">0.6404</td>
<td id="A4.T6.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.5491</td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T6.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.T6.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="A4.T6.2.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HiSAC</span></th>
<td id="A4.T6.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="A4.T6.2.5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.6444</span></td>
<td id="A4.T6.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="A4.T6.2.5.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.5525</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="A4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our approach on an industrial dataset with sequences of length 10k, as illustrated in Table <a href="#A4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Appendix D Comparison with Two-Stage Methods ‣ HiSAC: Hierarchical Sparse Activation Compression for Ultra-long Sequence Modeling in Recommenders" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. All two-stage algorithms first retrieve 256 user historical interactions during the GSU stage, and then interact these retrieved histories with candidate items in the ESU stage. The ESU stage uniformly employs MHA for modeling. As observed, due to discarding the majority of user interaction information in the two-stage retrieval process, these methods exhibit a performance gap compared to full-sequence modeling.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
